From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vipul Cariappa <vipulcariappa@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 20:38:59 +0530
Subject: Flush Python's stream buffers before ending the capture

`%%python` captured the raw file descriptors, but sys.stdout and sys.stderr
are block-buffered when those are not a tty, so everything print() wrote
was still in Python's own buffer when the capture ended -- and was then
discarded. Cells that reported results by printing them appeared to run
and produce nothing, while C++ std::cout from the same cell came through
fine.

Flush Python's wrappers and then C's before closing the capture.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 1867247959ec2a9b61095952238ff074e01403c9)
---
 src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp b/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp
index 22706aa..58e8ca9 100644
--- a/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp
+++ b/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 
 #include "pythonexec.hpp"
 #include "../xparser.hpp"
+#include <cstdio>
 #include <string>
 
 #include "Python.h"
@@ -37,6 +38,19 @@ namespace xcpp {
         
         PyRun_SimpleString(code.c_str());
 
+        // sys.stdout/sys.stderr are block-buffered when the captured fds are
+        // not a tty, so anything print() produced is still sitting in Python's
+        // own buffer. Ending the capture here would drop it -- flush both
+        // layers first, Python's and then C's.
+        PyRun_SimpleString("import sys\n"
+                           "for _s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n"
+                           "    try:\n"
+                           "        _s.flush()\n"
+                           "    except Exception:\n"
+                           "        pass\n"
+                           "del _s\n");
+        fflush(nullptr);
+
         std::cout << Cpp::EndStdStreamCapture();
         std::cerr << Cpp::EndStdStreamCapture();
     }
